{"id":1556,"date":"2020-12-02T11:28:53","date_gmt":"2020-12-02T19:28:53","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1556"},"modified":"2024-08-27T12:52:56","modified_gmt":"2024-08-27T19:52:56","slug":"codrone-blockly-jr-variables","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-blockly-jr-variables\/","title":{"rendered":"Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2cdd8\" name=\"6a22153e2cdd8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are variables? <\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Variables are a way to store information in a program, and they&#8217;re used most often to store values. If you&#8217;re playing a video game, there&#8217;s a variable to store your score, another one for your lives, and maybe another one for time. With your CoDrone, you might use them to store the value of a sensor, the speed of your drone, or maybe how far it will go.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Variables are great if you have a lot of values in your program! For example, you might have a program with five different places where you set the speed. Without a variable, you would need to change all five numbers. With a variable, you would only need to change the variable&#8217;s value. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2cef7\" name=\"6a22153e2cef7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Create a variable <\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To create a variable, go to the pink variables menu in Blockly and click on the gray Create variable\u2026 block. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1557&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You\u2019ll be asked to name your variable, and this can be whatever you like! Just make sure it\u2019s something that you\u2019ll remember and that makes sense with what you\u2019re doing. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1558&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you\u2019re finished creating your variable, the variables menu will have new blocks for you to try out!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1559&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2cf5b\" name=\"6a22153e2cf5b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Set to<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the blocks you\u2019ll see is set _ to, which will set your variable to whatever value you choose.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1560&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To use it, drag it to the top of your code and then go to the gray math menu. From there, you can just use a number value block, or you can use more complex blocks that will make your math teacher super proud of you!\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1561&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2cfac\" name=\"6a22153e2cfac\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Get the variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">When you need to recall your variable, or use the information from it, you can use the get block from the variables menu. In Blockly, you will usually end up attaching this block to a loop or conditional. However, you can also set your variables to get the values of other variables! This is useful if you program events that are dependent on each other.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1562&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try out the example code to see what happens! <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1563&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2cfff\" name=\"6a22153e2cfff\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Change the variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you need to change your variable\u2019s value, you have a couple of options. <\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To change it throughout the program, you can just type in a new value in the math block next to the set variable block. <\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want to change a variable\u2019s value in just one part of your program, you can use the change by block. Drag the block to where you need it in your program and type in how much you would like to change the variable\u2019s value by.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1564&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2d05d\" name=\"6a22153e2d05d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Put it all together! <\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use what you learned to change your CoDrone\u2019s colors! Program your CoDrone so if your variable\u2019s value is less than or equal to 50, your CoDrone\u2019s eyes will turn one color, and if the variable\u2019s value is greater than or equal to 51, your CoDrone\u2019s eyes will turn another color. The LED blocks are in the purple lights menu.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For an extra challenge, put all of that in a repeat for loop and have your program keep adding one to your variable 100 times. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Not sure what a repeat for loop is? Don&#8217;t worry! That will be in a later lesson. Just know<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that whatever blocks are in the loop block will repeat 100 times.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wondering why the big numbers are needed? Computers can count very quickly so if you use smaller numbers without a wait block, the change in your CoDrone\u2019s LEDs will be very quick.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program might look something like this:\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1565&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22153e2d0c4\" name=\"6a22153e2d0c4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Pin Point\u00a0<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your CoDrone to take off from a landing pad, fly in one direction, and then land on another pad. <\/span><\/p>\n<p><b>Rules:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You need to use variables in order for your CoDrone to move in one direction and land. <\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The starting place and the landing pad shouldn&#8217;t change once the challenge has started, but you can change your code after testing it out.<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Don\u2019t forget a kill switch!<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want to make the challenge more competitive, turn the target into a bullseye and give each section a<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">different point value. The middle of the board should have the highest number of points, and the edges of the<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">board should have the least amount of points.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 What are variables? [vc_column_text]Variables are a way to store information in a program, and they're used most often to store values. If you're playing a video game,&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-blockly-jr-variables\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1754,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":806,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1817,1763,1777,1737,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2108,2164,2123,2122,2117],"class_list":["post-1556","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-variables","module-junior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1556","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":5,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1556\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":12718,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1556\/revisions\/12718"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1754"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1556"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1556"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1556"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}